  "content": {
    "id": "p17-q5-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "5",
    "prompt": "Write down and then prove the contrapositive of each of the following statements:",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p17-q5-a-node",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Suppose $m,n\\in\\mathbb Z$. If $m^2(n^2-2)$ is odd, then $m$ and $n$ are odd.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "If $m$ is even or $n$ is even, then $m^2(n^2-2)$ is even.\n\nIn either case, express the product as twice an integer.",
          "worked": "Contrapositive: If $m$ is even or $n$ is even, then $m^2(n^2-2)$ is even.\n\nIf $m=2k$ for $k\\in\\mathbb Z$, then\n$$\\begin{align*}m^2(n^2-2)&=(2k)^2(n^2-2)\\\\m^2(n^2-2)&=2\\bigl(2k^2(n^2-2)\\bigr).\\end{align*}$$\nThis is even.\n\nIf $n=2k$ for $k\\in\\mathbb Z$, then\n$$\\begin{align*}m^2(n^2-2)&=m^2\\bigl((2k)^2-2\\bigr)\\\\m^2(n^2-2)&=2m^2(2k^2-1).\\end{align*}$$\nThis is also even, proving the contrapositive.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 92
      },
      {
        "id": "p17-q5-b-node",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Suppose $a,b,c\\in\\mathbb Z$. If $a$ does not divide $bc$, then $a$ does not divide $b$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "If $a$ divides $b$, then $a$ divides $bc$.\n\nWrite $b=ak$; then $bc=a(kc)$.",
          "worked": "Contrapositive: If $a$ divides $b$, then $a$ divides $bc$.\n\nSuppose $a$ divides $b$. Then $b=ak$ for some $k\\in\\mathbb Z$, so\n$$\\begin{align*}bc&=(ak)c\\\\bc&=a(kc).\\end{align*}$$\nSince $kc\\in\\mathbb Z$, $a$ divides $bc$, proving the contrapositive.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 68
      },
      {
        "id": "p17-q5-c-node",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Suppose $x\\in\\mathbb R$. If $x^3-x>0$, then $x>-1$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "If $x\\le-1$, then $x^3-x\\le0$.\n\nFactor $x^3-x=x(x-1)(x+1)$ and consider the signs.",
          "worked": "Contrapositive: If $x\\le-1$, then $x^3-x\\le0$.\n\nSuppose $x\\le-1$. Then\n$$\\begin{align*}x^3-x&=x(x^2-1)\\\\x^3-x&=x(x-1)(x+1).\\end{align*}$$\nHere $x<0$, $x-1<0$ and $x+1\\le0$. The first two factors have a positive product, so $x^3-x\\le0$. This proves the contrapositive, including equality when $x=-1$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 68
      },
      {
        "id": "p17-q5-d-node",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Suppose $a,b\\in\\mathbb Z$. If both $ab$ and $a+b$ are even, then both $a$ and $b$ are even.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "If $a$ is odd or $b$ is odd, then $ab$ is odd or $a+b$ is odd.\n\nTwo odd integers have an odd product; an odd and an even integer have an odd sum.",
          "worked": "Contrapositive: If $a$ is odd or $b$ is odd, then $ab$ is odd or $a+b$ is odd.\n\nSuppose at least one of $a,b$ is odd. If both are odd, their product $ab$ is odd. If exactly one is odd, their sum $a+b$ is odd. Thus at least one of $ab$ and $a+b$ is odd, proving the contrapositive.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 60
      },
      {
        "id": "p17-q5-e-node",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "Suppose $m,n\\in\\mathbb Z$. If $m^2(n+1)$ is even, then $m$ is even or $n$ is odd.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "If $m$ is odd and $n$ is even, then $m^2(n+1)$ is odd.\n\nBoth factors are odd.",
          "worked": "Contrapositive: If $m$ is odd and $n$ is even, then $m^2(n+1)$ is odd.\n\nSuppose $m$ is odd and $n$ is even. Then $m^2$ is odd and $n+1$ is odd. Their product $m^2(n+1)$ is therefore odd, proving the contrapositive.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 52
      },
      {
        "id": "p17-q5-f-node",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "f",
        "prompt": "Suppose $x,y\\in\\mathbb R$. If $y^3+yx^2\\le x^3+xy^2$, then $y\\le x$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "If $y>x$, then $y^3+yx^2>x^3+xy^2$.\n\nThe difference is $(y-x)(x^2+y^2)>0$.",
          "worked": "Contrapositive: If $y>x$, then $y^3+yx^2>x^3+xy^2$.\n\nSuppose $y>x$. Then $y-x>0$. Also $x^2+y^2>0$, since $y>x$ means $x$ and $y$ cannot both be zero. Hence\n$$\\begin{align*}y^3+yx^2-x^3-xy^2&=(y-x)(x^2+y^2)\\\\y^3+yx^2-x^3-xy^2&>0.\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore $y^3+yx^2>x^3+xy^2$, proving the contrapositive.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 76
      },
      {
        "id": "p17-q5-g-node",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "g",
        "prompt": "Suppose $m,n\\in\\mathbb Z$. If $m+n$ is even, then $m$ and $n$ have the same parity (that is, they are both even or both odd).",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "If $m$ and $n$ have different parities, then $m+n$ is odd.\n\nAn even integer plus an odd integer is odd.",
          "worked": "Contrapositive: If $m$ and $n$ have different parities, then $m+n$ is odd.\n\nSuppose they have different parities. One is even and the other is odd, so their sum is odd. This proves the contrapositive.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 52
      },
      {
        "id": "p17-q5-h-node",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "h",
        "prompt": "Suppose $n\\in\\mathbb N$. If $2^n-1$ is prime, then $n$ is prime.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "If $n$ is not prime, then $2^n-1$ is not prime.\n\nFor composite $n=ab$, factor $(2^a)^b-1$; for $n=1$, the value is $1$.",
          "worked": "Contrapositive: If $n$ is not prime, then $2^n-1$ is not prime.\n\nFor $n=1$, $2^n-1=1$, which is not prime. If $0\\in\\mathbb N$, $n=0$ gives $2^n-1=0$, also not prime.\n\nOtherwise a non-prime natural number is composite, so $n=ab$ for integers $a,b>1$. Then\n$$\\begin{align*}2^n-1&=(2^a)^b-1\\\\2^n-1&=(2^a-1)\\sum_{j=0}^{b-1}2^{aj}.\\end{align*}$$\nBoth factors are integers greater than $1$: $2^a-1\\ge3$ and the sum is at least $1+2^a\\ge5$. Thus $2^n-1$ is composite, proving the contrapositive.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 116
      }
    ]
  },
